 The backend binds only to `127.0.0.1` by default (configurable in `config.yaml`; see “Configuration”), on port `3789` by default. The backend builds with `tsc` into `dist/`; the frontend builds with Vite into `dist/web/`. Messages are stored only in memory (up to 500 per channel, deduplicated by id, with edits overwriting existing messages), without a database. Stickers, profile pictures, and received media are cached by the backend in an LRU cache (200MB by default) and served over HTTP on the same port.
 
-The OpenChat adapter additionally keeps a 10-minute in-memory history cache, bounded to 5 chats with 5000 messages each. Concurrent queries for one chat share one history walk, pagination anchors use message time and id, and live messages, take-backs, and reactions are merged. An expired cache reloads reaction state; media downloads are authorized only after the message's page has been delivered. Logging out or changing accounts invalidates pending queries so old-account results cannot enter the next account.
+The OpenChat adapter additionally keeps a 10-minute in-memory history cache, bounded to 5 chats with 5000 messages each. Expiration uses a monotonic clock, unaffected by wall-clock corrections. Concurrent queries for one chat share one history walk, pagination anchors use message time and id, and live messages, take-backs, and reactions are merged. Replacing an existing message also preserves time-and-id order; ordinary in-order appends do not re-sort the entire cache. An expired cache reloads reaction state; media downloads are authorized only after the message's page has been delivered. Logging out or changing accounts invalidates pending queries so old-account results cannot enter the next account.
+
+Cached history uses binary search to locate cursor boundaries instead of scanning the entire message list. An anchor no longer present in the retained window still selects older messages using the same time/id boundary.
+
+Member-name lookups and message delivery queues are scoped to one login session. Obsolete work starts no further lookups after logout; new-account messages do not wait for old requests or inherit their retry cooldowns. Already-issued external requests may still complete, but cannot update the next account's state. Failed lookups wait five minutes of monotonic elapsed time before a later request can retry; wall-clock corrections do not alter this delay.
+
+Sticker, avatar, sticker-package icon and LINE emoji CDN responses enforce their existing 2 MiB limit while streaming. Oversized declared lengths are cancelled immediately; missing or understated lengths are checked against the actual body, without fully buffering an oversized download. Message attachments retain the separate `limits.downloadMaxBytes` limit; HTTP caching, ranges and access controls are unchanged. Sticker-catalog metadata has a separate streaming limit of 1,000,000 decoded UTF-16 code units, not bytes, preserving valid multi-byte text and the existing ID-range fallback on overflow.
+
+CDN responses rejected by status or sticker Content-Type have their unused bodies cancelled to stop unnecessary transfer. Cancellation failures do not change existing missing-media or error results, image fallbacks, or metadata fallbacks. Authenticated downloads of message media and files also cancel non-success response bodies while preserving existing missing-media and error classifications.
+
+Concurrent sticker-catalog requests in one login session share the pending owned-product query and CDN metadata load, while each request still receives its own complete reply. Successful results keep the ten-minute cache, measured by monotonic elapsed time and unaffected by wall-clock corrections; failures can be retried by a later request. Logout detaches the shared work; old completions cannot affect the next account's cache or load, and start no further metadata batches. Already-issued requests may still complete. Existing quotas and the sticker-ID range fallback on metadata failure are unchanged.
+
+Sticker metadata and owned-product ID ranges stop scanning once the existing 200-entry output limit per package is reached. ID order, duplicate entries and subsequent packages are preserved.
+
+Read snapshots requested when the web page opens history retain their ten-second sharing window, measured by monotonic elapsed time rather than wall-clock time. Live read positions still merge during that window, and logout discards the old account's snapshots. In-flight lookups remain shared past that window; completion does not restart it, and failures allow the next open to retry.
+
+Pending uploads remain usable for ten minutes of monotonic elapsed time, unaffected by wall-clock corrections. Expired items are still pruned on insertion or lookup, successful sends consume them, logout clears them, and existing capacity limits are unchanged.
+
+Member-name lookup stops scanning candidates once it selects the existing limit of 100 eligible members in input order, avoiding full-list intermediate arrays. Deduplication, cached-member skipping, failure cooldowns and lookup concurrency limits are unchanged. Chats in the same login session share pending lookups for the same OpenChat member, and later batches skip profiles resolved while waiting. Each caller still waits at most five seconds. Completion or failure releases the shared entry; logout clears it and stale results cannot remove a new session's lookup. Administrator authorization checks are not shared through this cache.
 
